WASHINGTON, D.C. — Today, Congressman Michael Baumgartner (WA-05) issued the following statement upon the passage of the final Fiscal Year 2027 National Defense Authorization Act (NDAA).

“I am proud to support the passage of the NDAA and stand with the brave men and women who wear our nation’s uniform,” said Congressman Baumgartner. “This legislation strengthens support for our Fairchild servicemembers and their families by increasing their pay and enhancing housing, education, and access to childcare, while ensuring our military has the tools and capabilities needed to deter our adversaries and defend the American way of life.”

“I am also pleased the House approved my amendment to authorize a $5 million investment in Reactive Alloy Structure (RAS) manufacturing, a critical new munitions technology developed at Washington State University and manufactured in Spokane,” Baumgartner continued. “In defense, every ounce of weight requires expensive fuel to fly. This technology will make our defense systems lighter, stronger, and more efficient,” he said.

RAS Background:

Reactive Alloy Structure (RAS) was invented by Washington State University researchers under contract to DARPA (Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency) and is working with Applied Research Associates (ARA) to manufacture the munitions;

ARA recently opened an industrial high-bay facility in Spokane, Washington to support RAS manufacturing;

In testing, RAS has demonstrated all the key qualities of hardened steel while also generating significant blast effects;

It’s effectiveness at destroying electronic systems is an astonishing 250% increase over an equivalent, non-RAS charge; and

This effectiveness against electronics is especially important as US forces deal with an increasing quantity of enemy drones in combat operations.

The passage of the FY27 NDAA authorizes $1.15 trillion for the following national defense priorities including but not limited to:

Authorizes a 5-7% pay increase for all servicemembers and reauthorizes targeted retention bounces and special pays;

Invests over $1.8 billion for the construction, modernization, and optimization of new military barracks and family housing facilities;

Authorizes $226 million to design and build new Child Development Centers for non-base childcare and over $170 million for new Department of War Education Activity schools;

Authorizes almost $1 billion to counter drug trafficking and grants the National Guard the authority to procure equipment for counter-narcotics and drug interdiction operations;

Authorizes $39 billion to restore America’s arsenal of munitions, including Precision Strike Missiles, Joint Air-to-Ground Missiles, Naval Strike Missiles, ect; and

Cuts nearly $30 billion in wasteful spending at the Department of War, including over $12 billion from inefficient and underperforming programs.

The NDAA passed the House on July 22, 2026, by a vote of 216-212. The Senate is currently considering its NDAA bill. The House and Senate must then reconcile the two bills into one, and both chambers must pass that bill prior to it going to the President’s desk to be signed into law.

WASHINGTON D.C. — Today, Representative Ami Bera, M.D. (D-CA-06), Ranking Member of the House Foreign Affairs Subcommittee on East Asia and the Pacific, introduced the Strategic Technology and Resilient Alliances (STRATA) Act alongside Representative Young Kim (R-CA-40). This bipartisan legislation would establish a Critical Mineral Innovation (CMI) Initiative at the State Department, coordinating allied partnerships, technology deployment, and new commercial tools to reduce U.S. dependence on China.

China currently dominates global critical minerals supply chains, including roughly 70% of mining and 90% of processing capacity. The CMI Initiative would help the United States and its allies accelerate innovative technologies that strengthen supply chains outside of China’s control.

“Critical minerals power everything from semiconductors and advanced manufacturing to energy systems and defense technologies, but our dependence on unreliable supply chains poses a serious threat to our economic and national security,” said Representative Ami Bera, M.D. “The STRATA Act addresses this challenge by building stronger partnerships with trusted allies and partners to advance critical minerals research, innovation, and production. This will make our economy more resilient and reduce our dependence on strategic competitors. I am proud to work with Representative Young Kim on this bipartisan effort to ensure the United States continues to lead in critical minerals innovation and advanced technologies.”

“The Chinese Communist Party has spent decades cornering the market on critical minerals and using supply chains as a tool of economic coercion,” said Representative Young Kim. “We cannot wait another moment to address this challenge. The STRATA Act plays to America’s greatest strengths: innovation and our strong alliances. By launching a Critical Mineral Innovation Initiative at the State Department, sharing technology with allies, and expanding domestic recycling, we will build a secure supply chain faster and smarter. Economic security is national security and this bill delivers both.”

The STRATA Act will strengthen U.S. critical minerals supply chains by:

Building structured partnerships with allied countries to coordinate research, development, testing, and deployment through shared priorities, joint funding, and aligned standards;

Aligning federal agencies and allies on joint research, shared infrastructure and critical investments;

Expanding recycling and recovery of critical minerals from waste streams and end-of-life products to increase supply, improve efficiency, and reduce exposure to geopolitically sensitive inputs;

Spurring economic gains by supporting commercialization of technologies through pilot projects, early-stage financing, demand aggregation, and procurement tools to scale technologies and attract private investment;

Strengthening U.S. economic and national security by accelerating innovation, and diversifying supply sources.

The STRATA Act complements Rep. Bera’s DOMINANCE Act that passed the House earlier in June.

