WASHINGTON, D.C.— Today, U.S. Congresswoman Emily Randall (D, WA-06),sent a letterto Acting Administrator of the Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA), Robert Fenton, demanding answers to why FEMA continues to delay funds that were awarded to the Aberdeen-Hoquiam Flood Protection Project, and why the funding will be $12.35 million less than what was originally approved for the project. Randall also calls attention to concerning reports that the Trump administration has been diverting FEMA funds to the Department of Homeland Security.

“FEMA Region 10 approved their request at $47 million, but headquarters denied that amount and replaced it with $34.65 million of grant funding. To make matters worse, FEMA intends to disburse funds [in] September 2026, one year later than FEMA previously committed. This community has waited long enough. This is unacceptable and it is dangerous,” wrote Randall.

“It is equally concerning to see a new published report by the Ranking Member of the Subcommittee on Economic Development, Public Buildings and Emergency Management and his staff of July 2026 where it details FEMA, instructed by the Department of Homeland Security, diverted agency resources for immigration enforcement and not for its intended purpose of our country’s disaster readiness,” Randall continued in the letter.

The Aberdeen-Hoquiam Flood Protection Project was allocated $87 million in BRIC grants when FEMA abruptly cancelled the program in April 2025, putting the funds on hold. Rep. Randall immediately sent a letter to then DHS Secretary Kristi Noem demanding answers regarding the status of the funding. In December 2025, Judge Richard G. Sterns of the U.S. District Court for Massachusetts ruled that the Trump administration unlawfully ended the BRIC grant program.

Rep. Randall sent another letter to Noem in January 2026 urging FEMA to release the funding. FEMA announced they would relaunch the BRIC Grant program on March 18, 2026 only after a federal judge issued a court order on March 6, 2026, giving FEMA two weeks to comply with the ruling and reinstate the funding.

Congresswoman Randall is requesting answers to three questions from Acting Administrator Fenton by July 31st, 2026;

An updated timeline on the disbursement of the FEMA BRIC funding for the North Shore Levee Project;

A recommitment by FEMA to immediately unfreeze and disburse the money originally approved by FEMA Region 10 for the City of Hoquiam and the City of Aberdeen;

A third request for a meeting to discuss how FEMA intends to prevent further economic harm in Washington’s 6th District.

Washington, D.C. – Today, U.S. Senator Catherine Cortez Masto (D-Nev.) re-introduced the Fairness for Immigrant Families Act, a set of commonsense proposals aimed at fixing America’s broken immigration system and setting up a pathway to citizenship for immigrants who have spent decades living in and contributing to this country. This legislation addresses the outdated laws complicating our immigration system, cracks down on notario fraud, and sets up guardrails against detention and deportation for immigrants who deserve stability, like DACA recipients and parents of U.S. citizens.

“Hardworking, law-abiding immigrant families across the U.S. are looking to Congress to finally address immigration reform,” said Senator Cortez Masto. “While our immigration system has long been broken, I believe it can be fixed. That’s why I’m putting forth pragmatic, commonsense proposals to bring people out of the shadows in the country where they’ve built their lives and provide them true access to the American Dream.”

To support immigrant communities in Nevada and across the country, this legislation would:

Make it easier for long-term immigrants with no criminal record to become lawful permanent residents;

Criminalize schemes to provide fraudulent immigration services

Require high-level approval before any parent of a U.S. citizen child is deported and direct the State Department to create an office dedicated to assisting U.S. citizen children who have left the country to stay with a deported parent;

Prohibit deporting DACA recipients and certain immigrants with pending applications;

Establish grant programs so states have resources to crack down on notario fraud;

Require DHS to report detailed financial data, including how they are collecting and spending immigration fees, as well as up-to-date information on adjudication backlogs and delays; and

Protect immigrants who are victims of or witnesses to crimes or are defending civil and labor rights from enforcement actions.

Read the full bill here and a summary here. The Fairness for Immigrant Families Act is endorsed by UnidosUS, Voto Latino, Third Way, American Immigration Lawyers Association, American Civil Liberties Union, United We Dream Action, FWD.us, Democracy Power Project, America’s Voice, Refugees International, Nevada Immigrant Coalition, AILA Nevada, and Latin Chamber of Commerce of Las Vegas.

The first and only Latina Senator, Cortez Masto has consistently supported immigrant communities in Nevada. She has called on both the Biden Administration and Trump Administration to protect Dreamers, TPS holders and other immigrants. Cortez Masto has worked to pass meaningful immigration reform that balances critical border security measures with a pathway to citizenship for Dreamers. Earlier this month, the Senator attended and spoke at a naturalization ceremony for new American citizens at the Sand Harbor Amphitheater at Lake Tahoe, Nevada.

